  "description": "The BRUTE arm of the ray-cull comparison: byte-for-byte vector_light_bake_cull except for the Circinus run label. Run this against a build whose VectorLightMath.Build has no angular index -- i.e. main -- and compare its run document against the cull one. Separate file rather than a flag because the cull has no feature switch: its output is bit-identical to the code it replaces, and a flag would put a permanent branch in a pure core to buy a measurement. \n\nThe equivalence pins are the point of keeping the two files identical. vector_light_lit_area, vector_light_shadow_fraction and vector_light_verts are pinned to values measured on the CULLED build; this arm runs the un-culled one and has to reproduce them exactly. That is the live half of the equivalence claim, and the half the offline suite cannot make -- it runs on CoreCLR and this runs on Mono. vector_light_segments_per_bake and vector_light_bakes are pinned for the same reason one level down: two arms measured over different wall populations or different bake counts are not comparable however close their milliseconds land.\n\nONE ARM PER RUN, and the arm is the BUILD rather than a flag: the ray cull inside the visibility polygon has no feature switch, because its output is bit-identical to the code it replaces and a flag would be a permanent branch in a pure core bought for a measurement. So this file is run twice -- once against a build with the cull and once against one without -- and the two Circinus run documents are compared, the way the four bake arms already are. Label the culled run 'cull' and the unculled one 'brute'. \n\nTHE SCENE IS LIFTED VERBATIM FROM vector_light_bake_gated so the wall population is the one an existing measurement already used: 23 torch lamps in a granite room grid. That matters more here than anywhere else in the epic, because the cull's whole gain scales with how much wall an emitter can see -- offline it is 1.00x at eight segments and 7.5x at four hundred. vector_light_segments_per_bake is pinned for exactly that reason: it states the population the timing was measured over, and a run that drifts to an emptier scene would report the cull as worthless while measuring something else. \n\nEQUIVALENCE IS CHECKED LIVE, not just offline. The cull claims to change nothing, and the offline suite proves that bit-for-bit on CoreCLR -- but the game is Mono, whose transcendentals are a different implementation, and the offline suite is structurally unable to notice a difference there. vector_light_lit_area, vector_light_shadow_fraction and vector_light_verts are pinned TIGHT against their existing measured values for that reason: they are the live equivalence test, and they are the pins that should bite. The timing pins are recorded rather than asserted. \n\nEXACTLY ONE REBAKE, provoked by toggling penumbra off and back on, following bake_gated: the flag flips stop dirtying the map after the first whole-map rebake, so a second arm in the same process would record nothing while the instrumentation stayed live. The toggle returns the arm to the state it started in, so what is timed is a rebake OF this configuration rather than a transition between two. \n\nCLOUDS ARE OFF. They are not the subject, they drift on the tick counter, and TickLapse runs the clock -- so leaving them on would put a different sky over each arm's frames for no benefit to anything measured here. \n\nPARENTS JUDGE, CHILDREN APPORTION. circinus_bake_* arms VectorLightField.EnsurePolygon, which contains all three stages, and it is the only arm to compare across builds. circinus_poly_*, circinus_silh_* and circinus_cover_* break that total down and must be checked to sum to it before either is believed -- Circinus instruments by transpiling, so its per-call overhead inflates small methods, and a child arm on the very method being optimised is the case where that bites hardest.",
